.TH IP6 3
.SH NAME
ip6 \- IPv6 address support
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft CW
#include <ip6.h>

char*      fmtip6(const unsigned char* \fIaddr\fP, int \fIbits\fP);
int        strtoip6(const char* \fIstr\fP, char** \fIend\fP, unsigned char* \fIaddr\fP, unsigned char* \fIbits\fP);
.ft
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION
\f(CWfmtip6\fP
formats the IPv6 address
.IR addr ,
which points to a buffer of
.B IP6ADDR
(16) bytes, with optional prefix bits
.I bits
(appended as a
.BI / bits
suffix if 0 <=
.I bits
<= 128) into a thread-specific 0-terminated temporary buffer
and returns a pointer to the formatted value.
The longest run of zero 16-bit groups is compressed with
.BR :: ,
and IPv4-mapped
.RB ( ::ffff:a.b.c.d )
and 6to4
.RB ( 2002:a.b.c.d::... )
addresses are recognized and formatted with dotted-quad notation.

.PP
\f(CWstrtoip6\fP
converts a formatted IPv6 address from the 0-terminated string
.I str
into a host order IPv6 address in
.IR addr ,
which must be a buffer of at least
.B IP6ADDR
bytes.
Both canonical and
.BR :: \-compressed
forms are accepted, as are IPv4-mapped and 6to4 dotted-quad forms.
If
.I bits
is not 0 then an optional
.BI / bits
(prefix size in bits) is parsed and
.I *bits
is set to the number of prefix bits, or to 0xff if no prefix is present.
If
.I end
is not 0 then
.I *end
will point to the first unparsed character in
.I str
on return.
0 is returned on success, \-1 on failure.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
dss(1)
